    {
      "id": "c2",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Taking the root first keeps the arithmetic easy. 27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3² = 9, much simpler than 27² = 729 and then cube-rooting. Same answer, far less work.",
      "figure": "rad-power-root"
    },
    {
      "id": "i2",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Root first here too.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "What is 27^(2/3)?",
        "answer": 9,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 3,
            "feedback": "That's just ∛27. Apply the power 2: 3² = 9."
          },
          {
            "value": 18,
            "feedback": "Don't multiply 27 by 2/3. Take the cube root (3), then square it: 9."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "27^(2/3) = (∛27)² = 3² = 9."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "c3",
      "kind": "concept",
      "body": "Read the fraction as instructions: the **numerator is the power**, the **denominator is the root**. For 32^(2/5): the fifth root of 32 is 2, and 2² = 4. Denominator down (root), numerator up (power).",
      "figure": "rad-read-fraction"
    },
    {
      "id": "i3",
      "kind": "interactive",
      "body": "Fifth root, squared.",
      "widget": {
        "type": "numeric",
        "prompt": "What is 32^(2/5)?",
        "answer": 4,
        "tolerance": 0,
        "commonErrors": [
          {
            "value": 2,
            "feedback": "That's just the fifth root. Apply the power 2: 2² = 4."
          },
          {
            "value": 8,
            "feedback": "The fifth root of 32 is 2, then squared is 4 — not cubed."
          }
        ],
        "fallbackFeedback": "32^(2/5) = (⁵√32)² = 2² = 4."
      }
    },
